Modeling requirements:
• Derive the ontology structure from the combined input of standards, competency questions, and example data.
• Use the standards primarily to guide semantic alignment and reuse.
• Use the competency questions primarily to determine the functional scope and required answerability of the ontology.
• Use the example datasets primarily to ensure that concrete stress experiment data can be represented semantically.
• Create a coherent class hierarchy that balances conceptual clarity, standard reuse, competency question answerability, and data
representability.
• Reuse SOSA/SSN and schema.org concepts where appropriate.
• Define custom classes and properties only where the combined input requires concepts not sufficiently covered by existing
standards.
• Include meaningful rdfs:label and rdfs:comment annotations.
• Include object properties and data properties with clear domain and range definitions.
• Include OWL restrictions where they improve semantic precision.
• Use clear and consistent naming conventions.
• Avoid overly generic classes.
• Avoid unnecessary duplication of classes or properties.
• Avoid overfitting the ontology exclusively to the 3 example datasets; the ontology should generalize beyond the examples while
still being able to represent them accurately.
• Include the 3 example datasets as individuals where appropriate, so that the ontology demonstrates how the data can be
represented.
• The ontology should be suitable for later comparison with another stress ontology and with AI-generated ontologies based
separately on standards, competency questions, and example data.

Conflict handling:
• If the three input sources suggest different modeling directions, choose the solution that provides the best balance between
semantic correctness, standard compatibility, competency question answerability, and data representability.
• Do not simply merge all possible concepts. Prefer a coherent and non-redundant ontology structure.
• Do not prioritize one input source exclusively. The final ontology should reflect the combined influence of all three input sources.

Output requirements:
• Return one complete ontology in valid Turtle syntax.
• The ontology must be directly openable in Protégé.
